    "params": {
        "video_subject": "2 · The anchor price",
        "video_script": "Researchers asked experienced real estate agents to value a house. They toured it, saw the neighbourhood, and got the same information sheet. Except for one number: the listing price. Some agents saw a high listing price, others a low one. The agents who saw the higher price valued the very same house much higher. And most of them said the listing price hadn't influenced them at all. This is called anchoring. The first number your brain sees becomes a reference point, and everything after it gets judged against that number. It even works with numbers that are obviously meaningless. People who first spun a wheel of fortune gave higher or lower estimates depending on where the wheel stopped. That's why a jacket marked down from three hundred to one fifty feels like a bargain, even if you'd never have paid one fifty for it on its own. The first price you see isn't information. It's an anchor. And once it's dropped, it's hard to pull back up. Now here's a trick that uses a product nobody is supposed to buy.",
